topics.description:
CSS decisions directly affect accessibility.

### Focus styles
Keyboard users need to see where they are. Never remove outlines without a replacement.

```css
:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #2965f1; outline-offset: 3px; }
```
`:focus-visible` shows rings for keyboard use only.

### Contrast
WCAG AA: **4.5:1** for normal text, **3:1** for large text and UI components. Check with DevTools color picker.

### Respect user preferences
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: .01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: .01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}
@media (prefers-contrast: more) { :root { --border: #000; } }
@media (forced-colors: active) { .btn { border: 1px solid ButtonText; } }
```

### Visually hidden text (sr-only)
```css
.sr-only { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px; padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; border: 0; }
```

### Other points
- Use **rem** so text can scale; do not disable zoom.
- Do not rely on color alone (add icons/text).
- Tap targets >= 44x44px.
- `display: none` / `visibility: hidden` hide from assistive tech; opacity does not.
- Do not put important text in `::before` content.
- Keep line-height >= 1.5 for body text.

